          <h2>13. Intentional Consumption: Buy Less, Choose Better</h2>

          <p>At Nomadisch, we are interested in the relationship between design and everyday life.
          That includes the objects we bring into our homes — and the reasons we choose them.</p>

          <p>Lighting offers a particularly clear example of intentional consumption.</p>

          <p>A household does not necessarily need more lamps. It may need:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>A better-positioned light</li>
            <li>A more efficient bulb</li>
            <li>A warmer colour temperature</li>
            <li>A dimmable source</li>
            <li>A task light in the right place</li>
            <li>A fixture that works with the existing interior</li>
          </ul>

          <p>Replacing an unsuitable lamp with a carefully selected alternative may create a more
          meaningful improvement than purchasing several decorative products without a clear
          purpose.</p>

          <p>This is the difference between collecting objects and improving an environment.</p>

          <p>A good lighting purchase should answer a practical question: <strong>what problem will
          this light solve in my home?</strong></p>

          <p>If the answer is clear, the purchase becomes easier to evaluate.</p>

